course: dictionary
lesson: 5289
title: Junction
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A junction is a place where two or more roads, railway lines or paths meet or cross. You may see the word on a road sign, on a map, or in a safety lesson. «Stop at the junction and look both ways» tells you the junction is a place where roads meet, so you must be careful there.
explain: A junction can also be a place where things join, like wires or rivers. The context tells you which meaning is right. If the sentence talks about cars, lorries or paths, it means the first meaning. If it talks about wires in a circuit, it means the second.
explain: «You never ride up the inside of a lorry or truck at a junction, because the driver cannot see you and it may turn.» The clues are the turning truck and the driver who cannot see you, so a junction must be a place where roads cross. To check, swap in «crossroads» and read the sentence again: it still makes sense.
fact: A junction is a place where two or more roads, railway lines or paths meet or cross.
fact: A T-junction is where one road meets another road in the shape of a T.
fact: A railway junction is where two or more railway lines meet.
fact: On a hike, check the trail markers at every junction so you can stay on the right path and find your way back.
fact: In the UK, motorways have numbered junctions, so drivers can find their exit on a map.
exercise: «Stop, look both ways and listen at the junction.» What is a junction? | A map of the sea | *A place where roads or paths meet or cross | A kind of cake shop | A loud noise made by a car
exercise: «The junction in the circuit is where two wires branch.» What does junction mean here? | A place where cars must stop | A kind of road sign | *A place where things join, like wires
exercise: «A truck may turn across the inside lane at a junction.» Find and copy the word that names the place where roads meet. | lane | *junction | truck
test: «Motorways have numbered junctions in the UK.» What does junction mean here? | A small village by the sea | A numbered sign that shows the speed limit | A long, straight road with no turns | *A place where roads meet or cross
test: «On a hike, check the trail markers at every junction.» Why should you check them? | So you can buy an ice cream | *So you stay on the right path and can find your way back | So the walk takes longer | So you can count the trees
exam: Why should a cyclist never ride up the inside of a truck at a junction? | *Because the driver cannot see the cyclist and the truck may turn across the lane | Because bikes are not allowed on roads near junctions | Because the inside lane is always closed | Because trucks are too slow to notice a bike
